====================================================================
DOMAIN: Contemporaries and the Second International Context
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-11
PAPERS_FOUND: 15 total (High: 6, Medium: 7, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the philosophical and historiographical milieu surrounding
Kautsky within the Second International (1889-1914): the evolutionary Marxism
codified in the Erfurt Program (1891), Plekhanov's "monist" conception of history,
Luxemburg's dialectical-spontaneist account of capitalist breakdown and mass action,
Hilferding's finance-capital analysis, and the intellectual legacy of Engels's
late natural-philosophical writings (Anti-Duhring, Dialectics of Nature). The
generation shared a broadly naturalistic, developmental framework derived from
Engels, but differed sharply on agency, determinism, and the mechanisms of
historical change.

Plekhanov occupies a distinctive position: his "monist view of history" (1895)
synthesizes Spinozan materialism, Hegelian dialectics, and Darwinian
evolutionism into an orthodox "scientific Marxism" that closely parallels
Kautsky's own approach. Luxemburg, by contrast, stresses the spontaneous
self-organization of the proletariat and develops a theory of capital accumulation
that ties historical change to imperialism and breakdown rather than gradual
evolution. Hilferding provides the institutional-economic complement to this
landscape, analyzing how finance capital reorganizes capitalism at its "highest
stage" — a work Kautsky explicitly praised as a completion of Marx's Capital.
The revisionism controversy (Bernstein vs. Kautsky/Luxemburg) established the
key fault lines within Second International Marxism's philosophy of history:
could evolutionary socialism dispense with Marxist determinism and adopt
neo-Kantian ethics?

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
Understanding Kautsky's philosophy of history requires situating it within
the shared but contested framework of Second International Marxism, especially
the Engels legacy that all protagonists negotiated differently. Contrasting
Kautsky's deterministic evolutionism with Plekhanov's monism, Luxemburg's
spontaneism, and Bernstein's ethical revisionism makes visible what was
distinctive — and what was shared — in Kautsky's approach.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Mehring's historical-materialist historiography remains underrepresented in
secondary literature (no DOI-verified papers found specifically on Mehring).
Bebel's contribution to the shared historiosophical framework is almost
entirely undocumented in the secondary literature accessible through these
searches. INCOMPLETE entries noted below.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Secondary literature on Plekhanov (Sockol 2023, Rumyantseva 2025, Pavlov 2017,
Soboleva 2021) should be read alongside his primary texts; the contrast between
Plekhanov's Spinozan materialism and Kautsky's Darwinian evolutionism is
central to domain synthesis.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Orthodox Marxist determinism: 5 papers (Kautsky, Plekhanov, Engels tradition)
- Dialectical-spontaneist critique: 3 papers (Luxemburg, Lukacs reception)
- Revisionist / ethical socialism: 2 papers (Bernstein, Dale on Polanyi)
- Finance capital and imperialism: 3 papers (Hilferding, Howard/King, Gaido)
====================================================================
}

@book{plekhanov1895monist,
  author = {Plekhanov, Georgi Valentinovich},
  title = {In Defence of Materialism: The Development of the Monist View of History},
  year = {1947},
  publisher = {Lawrence and Wishart},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Plekhanov's foundational statement of Marxist philosophy of
  history, originally published in 1895 under the pseudonym N. Beltov. He argues
  for a "monist" conception of history in which nature and society are governed
  by a single set of material laws, synthesizing Spinozan substance monism,
  Hegelian dialectics, and Darwinian evolution. Social development follows
  necessary stages determined by the productive forces, and consciousness is
  entirely derivative of material conditions.

  RELEVANCE: This is the closest parallel to Kautsky's own evolutionary
  determinism among the Second International thinkers. Both Kautsky and
  Plekhanov drew on Engels's late natural-philosophical writings and both
  derived a "scientific socialism" that treats history as governed by
  natural-law regularities. Comparing the two reveals how shared Engelsian
  premises could be articulated differently (Plekhanov's Spinozan monism vs.
  Kautsky's Darwinian naturalism).

  POSITION: Orthodox Marxist determinism, monist-materialist wing.
  },
  keywords = {Plekhanov, historical-materialism, monism, Second-International, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{plekhanov1940materialist,
  author = {Plekhanov, Georgi Valentinovich},
  title = {Essays in Historical Materialism: The Materialist Conception of History; The Role of the Individual in History},
  year = {1940},
  publisher = {International Publishers},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Two essays presenting Plekhanov's mature historical materialist
  position. "The Materialist Conception of History" systematically expounds the
  base-superstructure model and the determination of consciousness by social
  being. "The Role of the Individual in History" addresses the tension between
  structural determinism and individual agency, arguing that great men can
  accelerate or retard historically necessary processes but cannot alter their
  fundamental direction.

  RELEVANCE: Directly relevant to debates about agency, determinism, and the
  logic of historical necessity in Kautsky's philosophy of history. Plekhanov's
  treatment of individual agency vs. structural determination mirrors debates
  in Kautsky scholarship about whether Kautsky's determinism leaves room for
  political will.

  POSITION: Orthodox determinism with a Spinozan-Hegelian inflection;
  contrasts with voluntarist tendencies in Luxemburg.
  },
  keywords = {Plekhanov, historical-materialism, agency, determinism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}
@article{sockol2023plekhanov,
  author = {Sockol, David},
  title = {Verso of the {``Vulgar''} Portrait: Georgi Plekhanov's Anti-Kantianism and Activist Aesthetic},
  journal = {Rethinking Marxism},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {35},
  number = {4},
  pages = {484--501},
  doi = {10.1080/08935696.2023.2251333},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Challenges the standard image of Plekhanov as a crude
  economic determinist by showing that his aesthetic writings were primarily
  polemical responses to neo-Kantian critics of Marxism. Plekhanov rejected
  the Kantian notion of aesthetic disinterestedness and emphasized class
  conflict as the formative force in artistic creation, developing an activist
  aesthetic that directly served revolutionary ends.

  RELEVANCE: The anti-Kantian polemics Sockol reconstructs situate Plekhanov
  (and by implication Kautsky) within the revisionism controversy: both opposed
  Bernstein's attempt to fuse Marxism with neo-Kantianism. Shows how the
  philosophical commitments of Second International orthodoxy were worked out
  through aesthetic and epistemological debate, not only political economy.

  POSITION: Rehabilitative reading of Plekhanov against "vulgar Marxist" label.
  },
  keywords = {Plekhanov, anti-Kantianism, aesthetics, revisionism, Medium},
  abstract = {This essay examines Georgi Plekhanov, founder of the Russian Marxist movement and a supposed exemplar of “vulgar Marxism.” It analyses Plekhanov's formulation of a Marxist aesthetic, interrogating the widespread image of Plekhanov's “vulgar” focus on economic determinism and his neglect of cultural forces' influence on revolutionary praxis, locating him instead within a fuller history of Marxist thought. A contextualization of Plekhanov's fin-de-siecle writings on art demonstrates that these texts were effectively a component of his contemporary polemics against critics of Marxist theory, and that within them he was pursuing his polemical agenda of criticizing Kantian philosophy and defending Marxist materialism. These dual aims manifested in Plekhanov's rejection of the Kantian notion of aesthetic disinterestedness and a stress on the formative role class conflict exerted on artistic creation, ultimately resulting in his formulation of an original aesthetic theory prescribing political commitment and thus mobilizing artworks towards revolutionary ends.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{rumyantseva2025plekhanov,
  author = {Rumyantseva, Tatyana Gerardovna},
  title = {Plekhanov as {``Defender''} of Kant from Neo-Kantians},
  journal = {Kantian Journal},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {44},
  number = {1},
  pages = {89--106},
  doi = {10.5922/0207-6918-2025-1-4},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Analyzes Plekhanov's polemics against German neo-Kantians and
  Eduard Bernstein, showing that Plekhanov paradoxically "defended" Kant against
  neo-Kantian misreadings while fundamentally opposing Kantian idealism. Calling
  neo-Kantianism (following Engels) "a step backwards" from Kant, Plekhanov
  argued it falsified Kant's doctrine to attack materialism.

  RELEVANCE: Directly illuminates the philosophical stakes of the revisionism
  debate within the Second International: Bernstein's turn to neo-Kantianism
  was perceived by Plekhanov (and Kautsky) as a philosophical betrayal. This
  article clarifies the specific arguments Plekhanov deployed in a controversy
  central to Kautsky's own interventions.

  POSITION: Orthodox Marxist materialism vs. neo-Kantian revisionism.
  },
  keywords = {Plekhanov, neo-Kantianism, revisionism, Bernstein, Medium},
  abstract = {The history of the reception and interpretation of Neo-Kantian ideas in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries shows the special role played by those who took a negative stand with regard to Neo-Kantianism and sought to dissociate it from and oppose it to Kant’s legacy. A prominent place among the latter was occupied by Georgy V. Plekhanov, most of whose works were fiercely polemical. Highly rating Kant’s works, in which he even found some coincidences with materialism, Plekhanov for a number of years engaged in polemics on philosophical and political issues with the German Neo-Kantians and Eduard Bernstein, who shared many of their views. Calling Neo-Kantianism, after Friedrich Engels, “a step backwards” from Kant’s philosophy, he argued that Neo-Kantians had misunderstood Kant’s philosophy and, in advocating a return to Kant’s theoretical philosophy in order to use it to refute contemporary materialism, had distorted Kant’s doctrine. Plekhanov’s so-called “defence” of Kant against Neo-Kantians should not be understood and accepted literally. Being a Marxist, Plekhanov tried to defend above all the views of Marx and Engels by revealing the flaws in the Neo-Kantian interpretation of Kant. Although he himself in some ways misinterpreted Kant, his views command some interest today because many of them played a certain role in the perception of Kant in the Soviet and post-Soviet times and were reflected in the discussions of the Kantian philosophy as a whole, especially its fundamental concept of the “thing-in-itself”.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{pavlov2017plekhanov,
  author = {Pavlov, Evgeni V.},
  title = {{``When Was Caesar Born?''} Theory and Practice of Truth in Plekhanov and Bogdanov},
  journal = {Stasis},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {5},
  number = {2},
  pages = {50--79},
  doi = {10.33280/2310-3817-2017-5-2-50-79},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines the intra-Marxist debate on the nature of truth
  between Plekhanov's "orthodox Marxism" and Bogdanov's empiriomonism. The
  central dispute is whether truth is absolute (Plekhanov) or relative and
  organizationally constructed (Bogdanov). Plekhanov defended a correspondence
  theory of truth grounded in materialist ontology; Bogdanov challenged the
  very notion of mind-independent reality.

  RELEVANCE: This epistemological debate within Russian Marxism shows the
  fragility of the "scientific" foundations of Second International orthodoxy.
  Kautsky, like Plekhanov, insisted on the objective, science-like character
  of historical materialism — understanding the Bogdanov challenge clarifies
  what was at stake in that claim.

  POSITION: Orthodoxy vs. empiriomonist revision within Marxist epistemology.
  },
  keywords = {Plekhanov, Bogdanov, epistemology, truth, Russian-Marxism, Medium},
  abstract = {Although political differences between various factions of the Russian Social­Democratic Labor Party (RSDLP) have been studied quite extensively, theoretical debates about the nature of Marxism and the role such debates had in determining Party programs and strategic decisions remains in need of a more thorough engagement. One such theoretical discussion, the question of the nature of (absolute) truth, is the subject matter of the present essay. The primary debate was between Georgi Plekhanov, one of the most respected representatives of early Russian Marxism, and a group of theoreticians united around Alexander Bogdanov, an early collaborator of Lenin (until their break in 1909) and an author of several original theoretical works that attempted to challenge the established orthodoxy of Plekhanovite Marxism. The question of the nature of truth stood at the center of the debates between Plekhanov and Bogdanov. The ultimate challenge that Bogdanov threw to the representatives of (self­proclaimed) “orthodox Marxism” was the question of the ultimate nature of Marxist theory: Is Marxism a science or a faith? If it is a science, it must operate within a scientific theory of truth and, as is the case with any genuine science, continue to grow and adapt to the changing circumstances of research. If it is indeed a faith, a set of absolute truths revealed to one person (Marx) and passed on to his disciples in sacred texts and by appointed prophets, then it contains as much value as any other faith or superstition.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{soboleva2021spinozism,
  author = {Soboleva, Maja},
  title = {Marxism as Spinozism? One Episode in the History of Soviet Philosophy},
  journal = {Studies in East European Thought},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {74},
  number = {3},
  pages = {319--332},
  doi = {10.1007/s11212-021-09427-0},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Bogdanov's critique of Plekhanov's attempt to
  identify Marxism with Spinozism. Bogdanov objected on methodological grounds
  (ignoring historical context of Spinoza's concepts) and systematic grounds
  (challenging the extension-thought parallelism). Bogdanov's alternative
  "empiriomonism" rethought Spinoza through a theory of collective cognition.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the specifically Spinozan dimension of Plekhanov's
  monism that Kautsky partially shared. Kautsky too adopted a naturalistic
  monism influenced by Spinoza through Engels; the Bogdanov-Plekhanov debate
  reveals the contested status of this Spinozan inheritance within Second
  International Marxism.

  POSITION: Internal critique of orthodox Marxist monism from within the
  Bolshevik milieu.
  },
  keywords = {Plekhanov, Bogdanov, Spinoza, monism, Soviet-philosophy, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper seeks to reconstruct philosopher Aleksandr Bogdanov’s approach to the philosophy of Spinoza in the context of the debate against Plekhanov. I demonstrate that the Soviet interest in Spinoza’s theory has never been purely historical, but rather, it served an important function in developing the theoretical foundations for Marxist philosophy. However, Bogdanov was one of only a very few who objected strongly to Plekhanov’s attempt to relate Spinoza’s philosophy to Marxism in a direct way. Two principles underlie Bogdanov’s critique: one being methodological, the other—systematic. The methodological principle has a hermeneutical character, since it demands that we treat historical concepts by taking into account their context and their changes during the time. According to Bogdanov, failing to fulfil this principle results in the dogmatization and instrumentalization of philosophy, and transforms it into political doctrine. The systematic principle concerns Bogdanov’s radical rethinking of the relationship between extension and thought. I argue that by rethinking Spinoza’s concepts in the framework of “ideo-empirical parallelism”, Bogdanov develops his own theory of cognition, which he called “empiriomonism”. When considered in historical context, I argue that these debates can serve as a window into the foundational role the Spinoza’s philosophy has played in the formation of different versions of Russian Marxism, as well as in the development of Russian Marxism in general.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{luxemburg2003accumulation,
  author = {Luxemburg, Rosa},
  title = {The Accumulation of Capital},
  year = {2003},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Luxemburg argues that capitalism cannot reproduce itself within
  a purely capitalist environment -- it requires non-capitalist "outside" spaces
  to absorb surplus value. This structural dependency on expansion into
  non-capitalist territories drives imperialism and predicts the eventual
  breakdown of capitalism once those spaces are exhausted. The analysis
  combines a critique of Marx's reproduction schemes with a theory of
  imperialism as historically necessary.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the most systematic alternative within the Second
  International to Kautsky's evolutionary optimism. Where Kautsky envisaged
  a gradual, law-governed transition to socialism through parliamentary means,
  Luxemburg's breakdown theory implies a catastrophic rupture driven by
  capitalism's structural limits. The contrast between the two is central to
  understanding what distinguishes Kautsky's philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Dialectical-spontaneist critique of evolutionary Marxism;
  breakdown theory vs. Kautsky's gradualism.
  },
  keywords = {Luxemburg, accumulation, imperialism, breakdown, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}
@incollection{bellofiore2004luxemburg,
  author = {Bellofiore, Riccardo},
  title = {{``Like a Candle Burning at Both Ends''}: Rosa Luxemburg and the Critique of Political Economy},
  booktitle = {Research in Political Economy},
  year = {2004},
  pages = {279--298},
  doi = {10.1016/s0161-7230(04)21011-2},
  publisher = {Emerald},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Challenges reductive readings of Luxemburg as a crude
  economic determinist or mass-spontaneist by showing that her economic theory
  and political perspective are richer and more coherent than usually recognized.
  Argues that Luxemburg's crisis theory is not underconsumptionist but rooted
  in inter-sectoral disequilibria and relative reduction of workers' consumption;
  her theory of party organization was also not anti-organizational but
  emphasized building class consciousness "from below."

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most philosophically careful secondary account of
  Luxemburg's economic theory and its political implications, showing how her
  breakdown thesis relates to her theory of spontaneous mass action. Essential
  context for distinguishing Luxemburg's philosophy of historical change from
  Kautsky's evolutionary-determinist model.

  POSITION: Rehabilitative reading of Luxemburg against both spontaneist
  and determinist reductions.
  },
  keywords = {Luxemburg, political-economy, breakdown, spontaneity, Medium},
  abstract = {Luxemburg’s legacy is customarily reduced to two “errors”: crude economic determinism, blind belief in the spontaneity of the masses. The paper reconstructs Luxemburg’s arguments about the tendency to the “final” breakdown of capitalism and her criticism of Lenin, and shows how her economic theory and political perspective are different and much richer than usually recognized. Building not only on the Accumulation of Capital but also on the Introduction to Political Economy, the paper shows that: (i) Luxemburg saw the internal link between value, abstract labour and money; (ii) she emphasized the connection between dynamic competition, relative surplus value extraction, and the “law” of the falling tendency of the “relative wage”; (iii) her theory of the crisis is not underconsumptionist. The shortage of effective demand is seen as ultimately due to a fall of autonomous investment caused by inter-sectoral disequilibria springing from the revolution in the methods of production and the consequent relative reduction of workers’ consumption. “Disproportionalities,” as soon as they affect important branches of production, end up in a general glut of commodities. The paper also assesses Luxemburg political views. Her theory of the party was very different than the one held by the Bolsheviks, but it was a view in which the organization was essential for building class consciousness “from below.” Thus, in the end, Luxemburg’s questions seems to be more interesting than her critics’ answers, her defeats more fruitful than her opponents’ victories. The paper also considers the relationship between the personal and the political in Luxemburg.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{gronow2016formation,
  author = {Gronow, Jukka},
  title = {On the Formation of Marxism: Karl Kautsky's Theory of Capitalism, the Marxism of the Second International and Karl Marx's Critique of Political Economy},
  year = {2016},
  publisher = {Brill},
  address = {Leiden},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004306653},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Systematic reconstruction of Kautsky's Marxist theory of
  capitalism in relation to the broader Second International and to Marx's own
  critique of political economy. Gronow shows that Kautsky's reading of Marx
  diverges significantly from Marx's own categories, especially on
  immiseration, accumulation, and the state. The book also contextualizes
  Kautsky's debates with Bernstein, Lenin, and Hilferding within the
  formation of a distinct "Second International Marxism."

  RELEVANCE: Essential secondary source for understanding how Kautsky's
  views on history and political economy relate to his contemporaries in the
  Second International. Gronow's comparative analysis is directly relevant to
  tracking convergences and divergences between Kautsky, Luxemburg, and
  Hilferding.

  POSITION: Revisionist historiography of Second International Marxism;
  critical reconstruction of Kautsky's theoretical formation.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Second-International, Marxism, political-economy, High},
  abstract = {Preface and Acknowledgements Introduction PART I: KAUTSKY'S MARXISM 1. Organised Capitalism, the General Cartel and the Proletariat 2. The Dispute over Revisionism 3. The Theory of Immiseration, Socialist Consciousness and the Intellectuals 4. Socialism as Science 5. The Capitalist Law of Appropriation: Kautsky's Interpretation of Karl Marx's Economic Thought 6. The Centralisation of Capital and Monopoly Formation 7. Imperialism and the Relation between Industrial and Agrarian Countries 8. Imperialism and Its Alternatives 9. Imperialism as the Last Stage of Capitalism 10. Theoretical Sources of Kautsky's and Lenin's Studies on Imperialism 11. Imperialism as the Truth about Capitalism 12. Parliamentary Democracy and Revolutionary Tactics 13. The Question of Democracy and Dictatorship: Lenin's Critique of Kautsky the Renegade PART II: MARX'S MARXISM 14. The Immanent Critique and the Natural Rights Theory 15. John Locke, Adam Smith and Karl Marx's Critique of Private Property 16. The Principle of Labour 17. The Theory of Increasing Misery and the Critique of Capitalism Conclusion Bibliography Index},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{king2010hilferding,
  author = {King, J. E.},
  title = {Hilferding's {Finance Capital} in the Development of Marxist Thought},
  journal = {History of Economics Review},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {52},
  number = {1},
  pages = {52--62},
  doi = {10.1080/18386318.2010.11682164},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Traces the reception of Hilferding's Finance Capital (1910)
  in the development of Marxist political economy, documenting how Kautsky
  greeted it as "a completion of Capital" and how Otto Bauer read it as a
  fourth volume of Marx. King situates Finance Capital within debates on
  imperialism, monopoly capitalism, and the political prospects for socialist
  transformation, showing its influence on Lenin's own imperialism theory.

  RELEVANCE: Directly documents how Kautsky positioned himself relative to
  Hilferding's contribution, and how the shared conceptual framework of finance
  capital shaped Second International discussions of the future of capitalism.
  Kautsky's agreement with Hilferding, and his divergence over "ultra-imperialism,"
  marks a key juncture in his philosophy of historical development.

  POSITION: Intellectual-historical reconstruction of the Hilferding-Kautsky
  relationship within Marxist economics.
  },
  keywords = {Hilferding, finance-capital, Kautsky, imperialism, Medium},
  abstract = {When Rudolf Hilferding's 'Finance Capital' was first published, it was regarded as a major event in the development of Marxian political economy. Two of the most important contemporary social democratic theorists greeted its publication with great enthusiasm. For Otto Bauer the book read like a fourth volume of Marx's Capital, while Karl Kautsky concluded that Hilferding had vindicated Marx's approach to economics. Kautsky described Finance Capital as 'a completion of Capital', which both supplemented and revised volumes II and III (Bauer 1909 - 1910; Kautsky 1910 - 1911). Otto Bauer (1881 - 1938) was one of the leading theoreticians of the Austrian socialist party, and himself the author of an acclaimed text on the Marxian interpretation of nationalism (Bottomore and Goode 1978). Karl Kautsky (1854 - 1938), sometimes described as 'the Pope of Marxism', had been the most influential theorist in the German socialist movement since the death of Friedrich Engels in 1883 (Geary 1987). Their praise was not given lightly.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{howard1989finance,
  author = {Howard, M. C. and King, J. E.},
  title = {Finance Capital and Imperialism: Karl Kautsky and Rudolf Hilferding},
  booktitle = {A History of Marxian Economics},
  year = {1989},
  pages = {90--105},
  doi = {10.1007/978-1-349-20112-9_5},
  publisher = {Macmillan Education UK},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Comparative analysis of Kautsky and Hilferding on finance
  capital and imperialism, tracing how both drew on Marx's Capital to theorize
  the concentration and centralization of capital, the merging of industrial
  and bank capital, and the imperialist drive for markets. The chapter also
  discusses their different conclusions about the political trajectory of
  organized capitalism.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the comparative economic-historical context for
  understanding Kautsky's philosophy of history at its most politically
  concrete: his ultra-imperialism thesis presupposes a specific account of
  how capitalism develops over time, which diverges from both Luxemburg's
  breakdown theory and Lenin's theory of imperialism.

  POSITION: Historical reconstruction of shared and divergent frameworks
  in Second International political economy.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Hilferding, imperialism, finance-capital, Medium},
  abstract = {The final quarter of the nineteenth century saw a number of important changes in the world capitalist economy and in the political relations between the Great Powers. Whereas the late 1860s witnessed the closest ever approximation to universal free trade, the trend thereafter was towards increased protectionism, together with growing monopolisation primarily through the establishment of trusts and cartels. World trade continued to grow more rapidly than industrial output, and between 1870 and 1914 there was a massive migration of people from Europe to the newly-settled areas of North and South America, Australia and South Africa. This was accompanied by the exportation of capital on a gigantic scale, increasing the overseas assets of the European countries from $6.5 billion in 1874 to $44.0 billion in 1913, and by the frantic acquisition of colonies in the ‘Scramble for Africa’ and elsewhere. How far this amounted to a distinct new stage of capitalist development remains controversial, for there had always been elements of monopoly in capitalist economies and the fastest growth in both commodity and capital exports came before rather than after 1875, while the ‘imperialism of free trade’ had allowed Britain (especially) to expand its formal and informal empire throughout the nineteenth century.1},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{gaido2016hilferding,
  author = {Gaido, Daniel},
  title = {Rudolf Hilferding on English Mercantilism},
  journal = {History of Political Economy},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {48},
  number = {3},
  pages = {449--470},
  doi = {10.1215/00182702-3638631},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Introduces and contextualizes an English translation of
  Hilferding's 1911 article on English mercantilism, published in Kautsky's
  Neue Zeit. The article situates Hilferding's historiographical work within
  the broader project of Marxist scholarship in the Second International period,
  showing how the analysis of historical economic formations fed into
  contemporary political economy.

  RELEVANCE: Demonstrates the shared intellectual milieu of Kautsky and
  Hilferding through their joint publication platform (Neue Zeit) and their
  common approach to using historical economic analysis to illuminate contemporary
  capitalism. Shows the concrete textual and institutional connections between
  the two thinkers.

  POSITION: Intellectual-historical contextualization of Hilferding's
  work within Second International Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {Hilferding, Neue-Zeit, Kautsky, historiography, Low},
  abstract = {In 1911 Rudolf Hilferding, the author of the famous book Finance Capital: A Study of the Latest Phase of Capitalist Development, published an article on the history of English mercantilism in Die neue Zeit, the theoretical organ of the Social Democratic Party of Germany edited by Karl Kautsky. The writing of the article was motivated by the publication, the previous year, of the third volume of Theories of Surplus-Value, containing Marx's analysis of the work of Richard Jones, as well as by the simultaneous appearance of a German edition of Thomas Mun's England's Treasure by Forraign Trade. This article introduces the present author's English translation of Hilferding's article on mercantilism, titled “The Early Days of English Political Economy,” previously available only in German, and contextualizes it in the framework of the history of Marxist scholarship.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{dale2014polanyi,
  author = {Dale, Gareth},
  title = {The Iron Law of Democratic Socialism: British and Austrian Influences on the Young Karl Polanyi},
  journal = {Economy and Society},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {43},
  number = {4},
  pages = {650--667},
  doi = {10.1080/03085147.2014.898821},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Polanyi's reception of Bernstein's "evolutionary
  socialism" and Austro-Marxism, arguing that both incorporated a deterministic
  fatalism about democratization even as they rejected economic determinism.
  The article traces how neo-Kantian elements entered Second International
  thought and how later thinkers (Polanyi) absorbed and modified them.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the Bernstein revisionism controversy from an
  unexpected angle, showing how the combination of evolutionary socialism
  and neo-Kantian ethics produced its own form of determinism. Provides
  comparative context for situating Kautsky's resistance to Bernstein's
  ethical revision: Kautsky preserved natural-law determinism, while
